### Paired t-test and Poisson/Binomial Test (2019 Only)

```{r}
# Paired t-test of weekday vs weekend for members
library(stats)

weekday <- df_2019 %>% filter(member_casual == "member", !day_of_week %in% c("Sat", "Sun"))
weekend <- df_2019 %>% filter(member_casual == "member", day_of_week %in% c("Sat", "Sun"))

t.test(weekday$ride_length, weekend$ride_length, paired = FALSE)  # Welch t-test (unpaired as riders vary)

# Poisson regression to see effect of gender and age on ride count
library(MASS)
df_agg <- df_2019 %>%
  count(gender, age, member_casual) %>%
  filter(!is.na(gender))

pois_model <- glm(n ~ gender + age + member_casual, family = poisson(), data = df_agg)
summary(pois_model)
```

#### 🔍 Insights:

- **Gender & Usage:** Males took more rides than females. Among members, the difference is substantial.
- **Age & Usage:** Younger riders (median age ~27–35) dominate usage. Age has a negative association with ride count (Poisson β = -0.0337).
- **Member Type Differences:**
  - Casual riders took significantly longer trips on average.
  - Members took many more trips, especially on weekdays.
  - T-test confirms members ride longer on weekends (p = 0.0126).

#### 💡 Strategic Implications:

1. **Engage Younger Riders:** Offer incentives for loyalty in younger age brackets.
2. **Leverage Weekends:** Promote longer leisure rides through weekend campaigns.
3. **Balance Gender Usage:** Tailor marketing to underrepresented genders to increase inclusivity and usage.
